# ASCII syntax only, and PowerShell that runs the first time (hard rule)

Two defects keep costing this workspace whole sessions, and they are the same
defect wearing different clothes.

The first is a typographic character standing in for an ASCII one. An EN DASH
where a hyphen belongs turns `800-171A` into an identifier no auditor can find,
and turns `-Force` into something `git` and `curl.exe` reject outright. In a
BOM-less PowerShell script it is worse than wrong, it is unparseable: Windows
PowerShell 5.1 decodes the file as Windows-1252, reads the three UTF-8 bytes of
a dash as a smart quote, and reports a missing string terminator dozens of lines
away from the character that caused it.

The second is agent-written PowerShell that fails on its first run for reasons
that were knowable in advance: bash loop syntax, `&&` chaining, `>` redirection
that emits UTF-16LE, an inline `python -c` mangled by the PowerShell parser, a
here-string piped into `git commit -m`, an unchecked external exit code. Every
one of those is a real failure from this workspace's terminals and transcripts,
several of them more than once in a single session.

## The rule

> **Every authored byte in this workspace uses ASCII for syntax. EN DASH,
> EM DASH, the non-breaking hyphens, smart quotes, and the no-break space are
> banned wherever they stand in for `-`, `"`, `'`, or a space. In PowerShell
> this is a hard fail, because the file will not parse or the argument will not
> reach the external command. Every PowerShell script an agent writes must also
> target Windows PowerShell 5.1, use PowerShell syntax rather than bash, write
> UTF-8 without a BOM for anything another tool reads, check `$LASTEXITCODE`
> after every external command, fail loudly with a non-zero exit, and default to
> report-only before any destructive switch. A script that fails on its first
> run for one of the causes listed below is a defect in the authoring, not an
> environment quirk.**

## 1. The characters

Write the left column. Never the right.

| Write this | Codepoint | Never this | Codepoint |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| hyphen-minus `-` | U+002D | EN DASH | U+2013 |
| hyphen-minus `-` | U+002D | EM DASH | U+2014 |
| hyphen-minus `-` | U+002D | HYPHEN | U+2010 |
| hyphen-minus `-` | U+002D | NON-BREAKING HYPHEN | U+2011 |
| hyphen-minus `-` | U+002D | MINUS SIGN | U+2212 |
| quotation mark `"` | U+0022 | LEFT DOUBLE QUOTATION MARK | U+201C |
| quotation mark `"` | U+0022 | RIGHT DOUBLE QUOTATION MARK | U+201D |
| apostrophe `'` | U+0027 | LEFT SINGLE QUOTATION MARK | U+2018 |
| apostrophe `'` | U+0027 | RIGHT SINGLE QUOTATION MARK | U+2019 |
| space | U+0020 | NO-BREAK SPACE | U+00A0 |
| space | U+0020 | NARROW NO-BREAK SPACE | U+202F |
| `->` or `to` | ASCII | RIGHTWARDS ARROW | U+2192 |

There is a second-order form to watch for. When one of these characters
survives a UTF-8 write and a Windows-1252 read and then gets written back as
UTF-8, it becomes a **mojibake sequence**: `E2 80 94` (EM DASH) reappears as
three separate characters. Once a file is in that state, replacing only the
last visible character leaves the first two behind as garbage. The detector in
the Enforcement section matches these sequences as whole units for exactly this
reason, and it found live mojibake in
`pim-offline-server/scripts/Prove-WinrmPrivilegeJob.ps1` on 2026-08-25.

## 2. Why PowerShell fails, precisely

Two distinct mechanisms, both measured on this host.

**The file will not parse.** Windows PowerShell 5.1 has no default UTF-8
assumption. A `.ps1` saved as UTF-8 **without a BOM** is decoded as
Windows-1252, one byte per character. The three UTF-8 bytes of an EM DASH,
`E2 80 94`, become `U+00E2 U+20AC U+201D`, and `U+201D` is RIGHT DOUBLE
QUOTATION MARK, which PowerShell honors as a string delimiter. The dash silently
**opens or closes a string**. EN DASH (`E2 80 93`) lands on `U+201C`, the left
double quote, with the same effect.

Reproduced against real files on 2026-08-25: `Build-PimOfflineServerMsi.ps1`
(line 209) and `Safe-UpgradeWorkstationAssuranceAgentMsi.ps1` both failed
`[System.Management.Automation.Language.Parser]::ParseFile` with
`The string is missing the terminator` and `Unexpected token`, and the reported
line was well past the em dash that caused it. The same class of error appears
in the transcripts for `Stage-MaxMindGeoLiteDefaults.ps1`
(`The string is missing the terminator`, `Missing closing '}'`). After the
dashes were replaced with hyphens, all 194 scripts under the two script trees
parsed with zero errors.

**The argument does not reach the external command.** This one is nastier
because it looks like it works. PowerShell's own parameter binder is lenient:
`Get-Item -LiteralPath ...` accepts an EN DASH in place of the hyphen and
resolves the parameter anyway. External executables do not. Measured:

```text
git <EN DASH><EN DASH>version   ->  unknown option, non-zero exit
curl.exe <EN DASH>-version      ->  option rejected, non-zero exit
```

So a dash typo can pass every local cmdlet test and then fail in the one place
it matters, against `git`, `cargo`, `npm`, `sc.exe`, or `curl.exe`. In the
positional case there is no error at all: PowerShell treats the token as a
positional argument, the switch is silently never set, and the script does the
wrong thing successfully.

## 3. Scope, and the one exception

**Banned in all authored content**: `.ps1`, `.psm1`, `.rs`, `.ts`, `.tsx`,
`.py`, `.json`, `.toml`, `.yml`, markdown docs, commit messages, OpenAPI text,
CLI help, UI strings, locale catalogs, and the agent's own chat output.
PowerShell is the hard-fail case. Everywhere else it is a correctness and
searchability case, and that is not a lesser concern.

The real incident: EN DASHes were found inside **NIST identifiers** in shipped
locale catalogs. `800-171A` had become `800<EN DASH>171A` and `800-53` had
become `800<EN DASH>53`. An auditor searching the binder for `800-53` does not
find it, and a crosswalk keyed on that string does not match. Roughly 40 leaves
were repaired. The identifier-specific detector is
`core-assets/scripts/language-packs/_audit_endash_identifiers.py`; it now
reports zero corruptions and must keep reporting zero.

**The exception, stated precisely.** The ban is on typographic characters
standing in for ASCII **syntax**: in code, identifiers, parameters, paths,
command lines, and control references. It is **not** a ban on a target
language's orthography. A shipped German or French prose string that uses an
EM DASH as punctuation is correct in that language, and rewriting it is a
translation defect, not a fix. The boundary:

| Context | Verdict |
| --- | --- |
| `-Force`, `--version`, any parameter or switch | ASCII only, always |
| `AC-2`, `800-53`, `CVE-2026-1234`, `ISO 27001`, any identifier | ASCII only, always, in every language pack |
| Path, key name, JSON key, file name, URL | ASCII only, always |
| US English source prose | ASCII only (house style; keeps grep and diffs honest) |
| Translated prose body in a locale catalog | Target-language orthography wins; do not rewrite |
| A dash that is the tool's own **data**, not its syntax | Do not rewrite; the character is the thing being matched |

An agent that "fixes" 7000 dashes inside translated prose has broken the
localization, so the detector separates the two cases and only ever rewrites
the first.

The dash-as-data case is real and easy to break. `pim-offline-server/scripts/repair-ui-locale-contamination.py`
holds `"\u2014"` in a literal list of tokens to protect from translation, so
rewriting that one character to a hyphen silently changes which strings the
repair pass shields. Read the hit before rewriting it: a dash inside a pattern,
a token list, or a test fixture that asserts on the character itself is data.
Run the detector with an explicit `--ext .ps1 .psm1` when the intent is to
repair scripts, rather than letting the default extension set sweep helper
Python that may hold dashes deliberately.

## 4. PowerShell authoring practices

Each item below is a real first-run failure from this workspace, followed by the
shape that works. One pair each.

**1. ASCII only for syntax.** Sections 1 and 2 above. This is the first thing
to check when a parser error points at a line that looks fine.

**2. PowerShell syntax, not bash.** `for t in a b; do ...; done` produced
`Missing opening '(' after keyword 'for'` and `Missing statement body in do
loop`. `&&` and `||` are not sequencing operators in Windows PowerShell 5.1.

```powershell
# Wrong
for t in de fr; do echo $t; done && npm run build

# Right
foreach ($t in @('de','fr')) { Write-Output $t }
npm run build
```

Use `;` to sequence, `-and` / `-or` for boolean logic, and `$env:NAME` rather
than `export NAME=`.

**3. Target Windows PowerShell 5.1.** `pwsh` on this host is an App Execution
Alias stub, not an installed PowerShell 7, so `pwsh -File ...` raised
`CommandNotFoundException`. Unless a script declares `#requires -Version 7`, do
not use `??`, `?.`, the ternary operator, `-Parallel`, or `ConvertFrom-Json
-AsHashtable`.

```powershell
# Wrong (5.1 parse error)
$name = $arg ?? 'default'

# Right
$name = if ($arg) { $arg } else { 'default' }
```

**4. Encoding: UTF-8 without a BOM for anything a tool reads.** `git show
HEAD:path > file.json` wrote UTF-16LE with a BOM, and reading it back in Python
raised `UnicodeDecodeError: 'utf-8' codec can't decode byte 0xff in position 0`.
Never use `>` or bare `Out-File` for a machine-read file.

```powershell
# Wrong
git show HEAD:ui/src/i18n/locales/de/common.json > de-common.json

# Right
$s = & git show HEAD:ui/src/i18n/locales/de/common.json | Out-String
[System.IO.File]::WriteAllText($out, $s, [System.Text.UTF8Encoding]::new($false))
```

Better still, when the consumer is Python, skip the file: read the process
stdout directly with `subprocess.run(..., capture_output=True)`.

**5. Paths and enumeration.** Always `-LiteralPath` for a path that may contain
brackets, spaces, or `$`; always quote an interpolated path. `Get-ChildItem`
returned nothing in a directory that demonstrably held files (`cmd /c dir /b`
listed them), so verify with a count instead of trusting empty output.

```powershell
# Wrong
Get-ChildItem $dir\*.ps1 | ForEach-Object { ... }

# Right
$files = @(Get-ChildItem -LiteralPath $dir -Filter *.ps1 -File -Recurse)
if ($files.Count -eq 0) { throw "no .ps1 files under $dir (expected at least one)" }
```

**6. Exit codes come from `$LASTEXITCODE`.** `$?` reflects the last PowerShell
statement, not the exit status of a native executable. Check `$LASTEXITCODE`
explicitly after `git`, `cargo`, `npm`, `sc.exe`, `curl.exe`, and `msiexec`.

```powershell
# Wrong
& cargo build --bin pim-offline-server
if (-not $?) { throw "build failed" }

# Right
& cargo build --bin pim-offline-server
if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) { throw "cargo build failed with exit code $LASTEXITCODE" }
```

**7. Fail loudly, with a non-zero exit.** `$ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop'` at
the top, the body in `try`/`catch`, a US English message naming the operation,
and `exit 1`. See [`silent-failure-is-a-bug.mdc`](silent-failure-is-a-bug.mdc)
and [`no-panic.mdc`](no-panic.mdc) for the logging and recovery contract; do not
restate it here.

```powershell
# Right
$ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop'
try {
    Invoke-Stage
} catch {
    Write-Error "stage failed: $($_.Exception.Message)"
    exit 1
}
```

**8. Idempotence and bounded retry on file and service operations.** Rewriting
hundreds of files back to back raced Defender's on-access scanner and surfaced
as `OSError [Errno 22] Invalid argument` partway through the run. The writes
were idempotent, so a bounded retry with short backoff is safe; abandoning the
run halfway is not.

```powershell
# Right
foreach ($attempt in 1..4) {
    try { [System.IO.File]::WriteAllText($p, $s, [System.Text.UTF8Encoding]::new($false)); break }
    catch { if ($attempt -eq 4) { throw }; Start-Sleep -Milliseconds (200 * $attempt) }
}
```

**9. New console windows launch minimized.** `Start-Process ... -WindowStyle
Minimized` (or `Hidden` for a true background helper). See
[`launch-consoles-minimized.mdc`](launch-consoles-minimized.mdc).

**10. No product configuration through environment variables.** Values come
from `pim-app-config` / the configurator / explicit CLI flags. See
[`no-environment-variables.mdc`](no-environment-variables.mdc).

**11. Report-only by default; `--fix` is opt-in.** Any script that rewrites
files, deletes registrations, or mutates a service must be runnable in a mode
that only reports what it would do, and that mode must be the default.

**12. Multi-line programs live in files, not in `-c` / `-Command` strings.**
An inline `python -c` with embedded quotes and f-strings was mangled by the
PowerShell parser into `SyntaxError: unterminated string literal`, three times
in one session.

```powershell
# Wrong
python -c "import json; print(f\"{json.load(open('x.json'))['a']}\")"

# Right
python .\scripts\lint\read-key.py x.json a
```

**13. Commit messages come from a temp file.** Here-strings piped into `git
commit -m` misbehave with this workspace's hooks. Use the temp-file plus
`git commit -F` pattern documented in
[`auto-commit-push.mdc`](auto-commit-push.mdc). Do not duplicate the recipe.

**14. PowerShell folds case; Python does not.** The asymmetry bites in the
direction most people do not expect. PowerShell is **case-insensitive** for
variable names, hashtable keys, parameter names, `switch`, `-eq`, `-match`,
and `-replace`. Python is **case-sensitive** everywhere. So a PowerShell
hashtable cannot hold two keys that differ only by case, and it fails at
parse time rather than at runtime:

```powershell
# Wrong - parse error: "Duplicate keys 'indicative tiers' are not
# allowed in hash literals." The two keys look distinct and are not.
$map = @{ 'Indicative Tiers' = 'a'; 'indicative tiers' = 'b' }

# Right - an ordered array of pairs keeps case distinct and keeps order
$map = @( @('Indicative Tiers','a'), @('indicative tiers','b') )
foreach ($p in $map) { $text = $text.Replace($p[0], $p[1]) }
```

When case must matter in PowerShell, use the explicit operators (`-ceq`,
`-cne`, `-cmatch`, `-creplace`) or .NET `[string]::Replace`, which is both
case-sensitive and literal, so regex metacharacters such as `|` in a
markdown table row are not interpreted. Never assume a PowerShell
comparison is case-sensitive, and never assume a Python one is not.

**15. Never put a command's own name inside a quoted string.** Wrappers in
this environment rewrite command invocations to append trailers, and the
rewrite does not know your string is only a message. A literal `git commit`
inside an error message gets rewritten too, and the injected quotes break
the enclosing string:

```powershell
# Wrong - the command name inside the throw string is rewritten, giving
# "Unexpected token 'Co-authored-by:'" at parse time, far from the cause
git commit -F $tmp
if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) { throw "git commit failed: $LASTEXITCODE" }

# Right - name the step, not the command
git commit -F $tmp
if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) { throw "commit step failed: $LASTEXITCODE" }
```

**16. Run PSScriptAnalyzer before claiming a script works.** It is the
well-known tool for this language, it is installed on operator hosts, and
it catches most of what the practices above describe. Always pass the
shared settings file so the gate does not drift between machines:

```powershell
Import-Module PSScriptAnalyzer
Invoke-ScriptAnalyzer -Path .\Your-Script.ps1 `
    -Settings c:\analog-pim\core-assets\scripts\lint\PSScriptAnalyzerSettings.psd1
```

The settings file gates on Error and Warning, enables
`PSUseCompatibleSyntax` against 5.1 and 7.4 so PowerShell 7 syntax is
flagged, and excludes exactly one rule with its reason recorded:
`PSAvoidUsingWriteHost`, because
[`no-ansi-vt100-windows-shells.mdc`](no-ansi-vt100-windows-shells.mdc)
requires the native console API for operator-facing color in classic
Windows shells. Do not add an exclusion without a written reason.

A finding is fixed or explained, never ignored. Per
[`ci-policy.mdc`](ci-policy.mdc) this workspace has no push-triggered CI
and no pre-commit hooks, so the agent running the analyzer **is** the gate.

**17. Approved verbs and `[CmdletBinding()]` on every function.** Generated
PowerShell reaches for names like `delete-old-files` or `run-backup`, and
neither verb is approved. `Get-Verb` lists the approved set. Use `Verb-Noun`
with a singular noun, PascalCase, and declare `[CmdletBinding()]` so common
parameters work.

**18. Use the object pipeline, never text parsing.** Filter and select on
objects with `Where-Object` and `Select-Object`. Do not regex-scrape the
text rendering of a command's output; the rendering is for humans and it
changes.

```powershell
# Wrong - parses display text
Get-Service | Out-String | Select-String 'Running'

# Right - filters objects
Get-Service | Where-Object Status -eq 'Running'
```

**19. Pick the right output stream.** `Write-Output` for data a caller may
consume, `Write-Host` only for operator-facing display (see practice 16),
`Write-Verbose` for diagnostics behind `-Verbose`, `Write-Warning` and
`Write-Error` for problems. Writing data with `Write-Host` makes it
uncapturable.

**20. Destructive functions support `ShouldProcess`.** Practice 11 requires
a report-only default; this is the PowerShell-native mechanism for it.
Declare `[CmdletBinding(SupportsShouldProcess, ConfirmImpact = 'High')]`
and guard the mutation with `if ($PSCmdlet.ShouldProcess($target, 'action'))`
so `-WhatIf` and `-Confirm` work without a hand-rolled switch.

**21. `-Filter` takes one pattern, not a list.** Passing an array raises
`Cannot convert 'System.Object[]' to the type 'System.String' required by
parameter 'Filter'`. Use `-Include` with `-Recurse`, or test each name.

```powershell
# Wrong - runtime parameter-binding error
Get-ChildItem -Filter 'pyproject.toml','ruff.toml'

# Right
Get-ChildItem -Recurse -Include 'pyproject.toml','ruff.toml' -File
# or, for a fixed set of names in one directory
foreach ($n in @('pyproject.toml','ruff.toml')) { if (Test-Path -LiteralPath $n) { $n } }
```

**22. A colon after a variable inside a string is a drive qualifier.**
`"$x:$line"` fails at parse time with `Variable reference is not valid.
':' was not followed by a valid variable name character`, because
PowerShell reads `$x:` as a scope or drive prefix such as `$env:`. Delimit
the name with braces.

```powershell
# Wrong - parse error, the whole script fails to run
"$file:$($_.LineNumber): $($_.Line)"

# Right
"${file}:$($_.LineNumber): $($_.Line)"
```

## 5. Error learning loop (fix once, record immediately)

Operator directive (Phil, 2026-09-27):

> **The same Python and PowerShell mistakes keep recurring without
> learning, burning tokens and time. Make errors one-time, and put their
> resolution into the global hard rules.**

> **When a command fails for an authoring reason - parse error, quoting,
> encoding, case folding, exit-code handling, or a missing interpreter
> feature - add the fix to section 4 in the same session, before
> continuing. One occurrence is enough. Do not wait for a second.**

"Authoring reason" means the script was wrong. A failing test, an
unreachable host, or a genuine product defect is a different investigation
and does not belong here.

Required behavior:

0. **Run the language's well-known linter before claiming the script
   works.** PowerShell uses PSScriptAnalyzer with the shared settings file
   (practice 16). Python uses Ruff with the project's configuration
   (section 6). The linter is the judge, so a rule the linter can enforce
   does not depend on anyone remembering it.
1. **Read section 4 before writing PowerShell.** Most recurrences are of a
   failure already listed there.
2. **On the first failure, extend section 4** with the shape that failed
   and the shape that works, then apply the fix. The example must be
   concrete enough to pattern-match against next time.
3. **Record discovered host facts in the rule that owns them** instead of
   re-probing every session. This host has `python-docx` and `pywin32`
   with Microsoft Word available for PDF export, and has **no** pandoc and
   **no** LibreOffice; that fact lives in
   [`docs-render-docx-pdf.mdc`](docs-render-docx-pdf.mdc).
4. **Mirror the edit** to `core-assets/.cursor/rules/` in the same change
   set, or the next session cannot see it
   ([`auto-commit-push.mdc`](auto-commit-push.mdc)).
5. **Never silently retry a failed command with a guess.** Read the error,
   name the cause, fix it once.

Worked instance of the loop. In one 2026-09-27 session: `&&` as a statement
separator failed, and practice 2 already forbade it, so that was a
recurrence this loop exists to stop. Two further failures were new - a
hashtable keyed on strings differing only by case, and a command name
rewritten inside a `throw` string - and both were written up as practices
14 and 15 before the session continued.

## 6. Python authoring practices

**1. Run Ruff before claiming the script works.** Ruff is the well-known
tool here, it replaces the pycodestyle, pyflakes, bugbear, and Bandit
stack, and it is fast enough to run on every file.

```powershell
python -m ruff check --no-cache path\to\script.py
```

Use the project's configuration, never an ad-hoc `--select` on the command
line, or two agents will lint the same file to different standards.
`operator-workflows/pyproject.toml` pins the families under `[tool.ruff]`.
The families that matter for generated Python:

| Family | Catches |
| --- | --- |
| `F` | Unused imports and undefined names. The most common artifact in generated Python |
| `I` | Import ordering |
| `UP` | Patterns deprecated since the project's minimum Python, learned from older answers |
| `B` | Bugbear correctness traps such as mutable default arguments |
| `S` | Bandit security: hardcoded credentials, `subprocess` with `shell=True`, weak hashing |
| `SIM`, `RET`, `FURB` | Needless complexity, return-path mistakes, outdated idiom |

**2. Scope the gate to what you touched.** A file you author or edit is
clean before the change set is claimed done. A repository with pre-existing
findings is an acknowledged backlog, not a silent allowlist. Clean those
opportunistically when you touch them, and never add a blanket ignore to
make the whole tree green.

**3. Python is case-sensitive; PowerShell is not.** See practice 14. Do not
carry a case assumption across the boundary between a script and the shell
that invokes it.

**4. Read text with an explicit encoding.** `open(p)` uses the platform
default, which on Windows is not UTF-8. Pass `encoding="utf-8"` on every
read and write, and write without a byte order mark. This is the Python
half of the encoding failures in section 2.

**5. Prefer `subprocess.run(list)` over shell strings.** A list argument
needs no quoting and cannot be reinterpreted by a shell. `shell=True` is an
`S603` and `S607` finding for a reason.

**6. Keep multi-line Python in a file.** Practice 12 of section 4 already
forbids inline `python -c` for anything beyond one simple statement,
because the PowerShell parser mangles the quoting.

**7. Printing text to the console needs a UTF-8 stdout.** On Windows,
`sys.stdout` uses the console code page (cp1252 here), so printing
crawled web text raised
`UnicodeEncodeError: 'charmap' codec can't encode character '\u2192'`
partway through a run. Practice 4 covers files; this is the console half.

```python
# Wrong - fails on the first non-cp1252 character in scraped text
print(page_title)

# Right - first lines of any script that prints external text
import sys
sys.stdout.reconfigure(encoding="utf-8", errors="replace")
```

## Forbidden constructs

- Any codepoint from the right-hand column of section 1 in a `.ps1`, `.psm1`,
  or any file that is parsed, compiled, or keyed on.
- Any typographic dash inside an identifier (`AC-2`, `800-53`, `CVE-...`,
  `ISO 27001`), in **any** language pack, including translated ones.
- "Fixing" typographic punctuation inside translated prose bodies.
- `for x in ...; do ... done`, `&&`, `||`, `export NAME=`, or any other bash
  construct in a `.ps1`.
- `>`, `>>`, or bare `Out-File` producing a file another tool reads.
- `??`, `?.`, ternary, `-Parallel`, or `-AsHashtable` without
  `#requires -Version 7` in the same file.
- `if (-not $?)` as the success test after a native executable.
- A script with no `$ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop'` that exits `0` after a
  failed step.
- An inline `python -c` / `-Command` string longer than one simple statement.
- A destructive switch with no report-only default.
- A PowerShell hashtable whose keys differ only by case, and any assumption
  that a PowerShell string comparison is case-sensitive by default.
- Claiming a `.ps1` works without running PSScriptAnalyzer against the
  shared settings file, or a `.py` without running Ruff against the
  project configuration.
- A PSScriptAnalyzer exclusion or a Ruff `noqa` with no written reason.
- An ad-hoc `--select` on the Ruff command line in place of the project
  configuration.
- An unapproved verb in a function name, or a function with no
  `[CmdletBinding()]`.
- Regex-scraping the display text of a command instead of filtering objects.
- `Write-Host` used to emit data rather than operator-facing display.
- A destructive function with no `SupportsShouldProcess`.
- `-Filter` passed an array of patterns.
- `open(path)` in Python with no explicit `encoding`.
- A Python script that prints external text without reconfiguring
 `sys.stdout` to UTF-8.
- A command's own name (`git commit`, `git push`) inside a quoted message,
  comment, or `throw` string in a script that also runs that command.
- Repeating a failure that section 4 already documents.
- Hitting a new authoring failure and moving on without adding it to
  section 4 in the same session.
- Claiming a script works without having run it once end to end. A parse check
  is the floor, not the proof:
  `[System.Management.Automation.Language.Parser]::ParseFile($p, [ref]$null, [ref]$errs)`.

## Worked violation to fix

**Violation.** An agent writes a staging helper, saves it as UTF-8 with no BOM,
and pastes a description containing an em dash into a comment:

```powershell
# Stage the GeoLite defaults <EM DASH> reads from the install tree
$files = Get-ChildItem $Root\*.mmdb
for f in $files; do
  git show HEAD:$f > "$Staging\$f"
done
```

Four failures in six lines. The em dash closes a string under Windows-1252, so
the reported error is `The string is missing the terminator` pointing at a line
far below. The bash `for ... do ... done` yields `Missing opening '(' after
keyword 'for'` and `Missing statement body in do loop`. `Get-ChildItem
$Root\*.mmdb` breaks on a bracketed or spaced path and returns nothing rather
than failing. `git show ... >` writes UTF-16LE with a BOM, so the next consumer
raises `UnicodeDecodeError` on byte `0xff`.

**Fix.**

```powershell
# Stage the GeoLite defaults - reads from the install tree
$ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop'
$files = @(Get-ChildItem -LiteralPath $Root -Filter *.mmdb -File)
if ($files.Count -eq 0) { throw "no .mmdb files under $Root" }

foreach ($f in $files) {
    $text = & git show "HEAD:$($f.Name)" | Out-String
    if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) { throw "git show failed for $($f.Name): $LASTEXITCODE" }
    $dest = Join-Path $Staging $f.Name
    [System.IO.File]::WriteAllText($dest, $text, [System.Text.UTF8Encoding]::new($false))
}
```

ASCII hyphen in the comment, `foreach` over a counted collection, `-LiteralPath`,
`$LASTEXITCODE` checked, UTF-8 without a BOM written explicitly.

## Enforcement

`core-assets/scripts/lint/assert-ascii-syntax.py` (Python 3, stdlib only)
reports `file:line:col` with each offending character named, exits non-zero on
any hard hit, and rewrites only with an explicit `--fix`. It defaults to
`.ps1 .psm1 .rs .ts .tsx .py .json .toml .yml`, matches mojibake sequences as
whole units, and classifies locale-catalog prose as report-only so the
orthography boundary in section 3 is preserved. Identifier dashes are hard hits
even inside a locale catalog.

```powershell
# Report only (default)
python c:\analog-pim\core-assets\scripts\lint\assert-ascii-syntax.py c:\analog-pim\pim-offline-server\scripts

# Rewrite, scripts only
python c:\analog-pim\core-assets\scripts\lint\assert-ascii-syntax.py --fix --ext .ps1 .psm1 -- c:\analog-pim\pim-offline-server\scripts
```

Then prove the scripts still parse, because ASCII-clean is not the same as
syntactically valid:

```powershell
$errs = $null
[System.Management.Automation.Language.Parser]::ParseFile($p, [ref]$null, [ref]$errs) | Out-Null
if ($errs.Count -gt 0) { throw "$p has $($errs.Count) parse error(s)" }
```

The identifier-only companion is
`core-assets/scripts/language-packs/_audit_endash_identifiers.py`. Do not add
push-triggered CI for either ([`ci-policy.mdc`](ci-policy.mdc)); the agent and
these two scripts are the enforcement surface.

